LD(1)                          P32W Unix                           LD(1)

NAME
       ld - link editor

SYNOPSIS
       ld [-o out] [-e sym] [-M map] [-s] [--format aout|bin]
          [--text addr] [--data addr] file.o ... [lib.a ...]

DESCRIPTION
       ld joins relocatable objects made by as(1) and cc(1) into an
       executable a.out (or the file named by -o), resolving every
       reference between them. Each argument is an object or an
       archive made by ar(1); ld tells them apart by their contents.

       Objects are always loaded, in the order given. Then the
       archives are searched: a member is taken if it defines a
       global symbol that a loaded object refers to and that is not
       yet defined. Every archive is searched, members in name order,
       again and again until a pass takes nothing more, so the order
       of archives on the command line (and their position relative
       to the objects) does not matter.

       A program for P32W Unix is normally linked as

              ld -o prog /usr/lib/crt0.o prog.o /usr/lib/libc.a

       which is what cc(1) does.

LAYOUT
       Each object's .text, .data and .bss is placed after the
       previous object's, aligned to 4. In an a.out, text starts at
       0x1000 (page 0 stays unmapped, so a null pointer faults), data
       starts at the next 4096-byte page boundary after the text, and
       bss follows the data. The a.out has a 32-byte header (magic
       0x50333277, text size, data size, bss size, entry address, 0,
       symbol table size, 0), then the text, the data and the symbol
       table; see a.out(5). The output is made executable (mode 0777
       less the umask).

       ld defines these symbols, all addresses: _text, _etext (end of
       text), _data, _edata (end of data), _data_load (where the data
       is stored in the file image), _bss and _end (the first free
       byte after bss, a multiple of 4).

SYMBOL TABLE
       Unless -s is given, the a.out ends with a symbol table for
       db(1): every symbol of every object linked, global and local,
       and ld's own, sorted by address. Each entry is the value (4
       bytes), a type letter (T text, D data, B bss, A absolute;
       lower case for a local symbol), a length byte and the name,
       padded to a multiple of 4. exec(2) ignores it.

OPTIONS
       -o out  name the output (default a.out).
       -e sym  the entry point (default _start). It must be defined.
       -M map  write a link map to the file map: the section ranges,
              each input's text, data and bss addresses, and every
              global symbol by address.
       -s     leave out the symbol table.
       --format aout|bin
              aout (the default) makes a P32W Unix executable. bin
              makes a flat image with no header and no symbol table,
              for a boot ROM or a stand-alone RAM load: text at
              address 0 (or --text), data running straight after the
              text (or at --data) but stored after the text in the
              image, so start-up code must copy it from _data_load.
              The entry symbol is not needed and not stored.
       --text addr
              link the text at addr (0x hex, 0 octal or decimal).
       --data addr
              link the data at addr.

EXAMPLES
       ld -o hello /usr/lib/crt0.o hello.o /usr/lib/libc.a
       ld -s -o prog /usr/lib/crt0.o a.o b.o /usr/lib/libc.a
       ld -M prog.map -o prog /usr/lib/crt0.o prog.o /usr/lib/libc.a

DIAGNOSTICS
       ld: undefined: sym (in file.s) for each reference that nothing
       defines (all are listed); ld: sym defined twice (a and b);
       ld: entry symbol sym is not defined; ld: a jump to sym is out
       of range or unaligned (a JMP or CALL reaches +-32 MiB); also
       cannot read file, file: not a P32W object or archive, file: a
       damaged archive, nothing to link, unknown option. In every case
       no output is written.

EXIT STATUS
       0 on success, 1 on any error.

SEE ALSO
       as(1), cc(1), ar(1), db(1), a.out(5), ar(5)

BUGS
       There are no -l, -L, -r (incremental linking), -x, -u or -T
       options: libraries are named as files, and the output cannot
       be linked again. There are no common symbols; a global defined
       in two objects is an error.

       Only the binary object format (version 2) is read; the host
       linker also reads the older JSON objects.

       The symbol table name length is a byte, so longer names are
       cut to 255 characters.
